---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import CalculatorCard from "../components/CalculatorCard.astro";
import { calculators } from "../data/calculators.js";

const travelCalculators = calculators.filter(
  (calculator) => calculator.category === "Travel"
);
---

<Layout title="Travel Budget Calculators | ShouldISpend">
  <style>
    .container {
      max-width: 1100px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 80px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: 4rem;
      line-height: 1;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      max-width: 850px;
    }

    .intro {
      max-width: 760px;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
      line-height: 1.8;
      margin-bottom: 56px;
    }

    .grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(320px, 1fr));
      gap: 24px;
      margin-top: 32px;
    }

    .content-section {
      max-width: 780px;
      margin-top: 80px;
    }

    .content-section h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      margin-bottom: 20px;
    }

    .content-section h3 {
      font-size: 1.3rem;
      margin-top: 34px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    .content-section p,
    .content-section li {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.8;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    .link-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 16px;
      margin: 28px 0 18px;
    }

    .link-card {
      display: block;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 18px;
      padding: 18px;
      background: var(--card);
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
    }

    .link-card strong {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .link-card span {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.6;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
    }

    .faq-item {
      margin-top: 26px;
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">Travel Calculators</div>

    <h1>Travel Budget Calculators and Vacation Affordability Tools</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      Travel can be one of the most worthwhile uses of money, but the final cost often
      reaches beyond flights and hotels. These calculators help you decide whether a
      vacation fits your income, savings, debt, emergency cushion, and long-term financial
      flexibility before you book.
    </p>

    <div class="grid">
      {travelCalculators.map((calculator) => (
        <CalculatorCard
          category={calculator.category}
          title={calculator.title}
          description={calculator.description}
          href={calculator.href}
        />
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Popular Travel Affordability Calculators</h2>

      <p>
        Different trips create different kinds of financial pressure. A Disney vacation may
        be driven by tickets, park days, food, and souvenirs. A cruise may look bundled until
        gratuities, excursions, drink packages, and flights are included. A Europe trip may
        require trains, hotels, museums, meals, and an exchange-rate cushion.
      </p>

      <div class="link-grid">
        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-5000-on-a-vacation">
          <strong>$5,000 Vacation Calculator</strong>
          <span>Estimate whether a standard vacation fits your current budget.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-8000-on-a-disney-vacation">
          <strong>$8,000 Disney Vacation Calculator</strong>
          <span>Check tickets, hotels, food, souvenirs, and park-day pressure.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-10000-on-a-europe-trip">
          <strong>$10,000 Europe Trip Calculator</strong>
          <span>Evaluate flights, hotels, trains, museums, meals, and currency buffers.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-12000-on-a-cruise">
          <strong>$12,000 Cruise Calculator</strong>
          <span>Account for gratuities, excursions, drink packages, flights, and port costs.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-15000-on-a-honeymoon">
          <strong>$15,000 Honeymoon Calculator</strong>
          <span>Balance milestone memories with wedding debt and post-wedding cash flow.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-20000-on-a-family-vacation">
          <strong>$20,000 Family Vacation Calculator</strong>
          <span>Measure airfare multipliers, larger rooms, meals, activities, and kid costs.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>How Much Should You Spend on Travel?</h2>

      <p>
        A travel budget should be judged by more than the trip price. The better question is
        whether the trip still works after your <a href="/how-much-emergency-savings-should-i-have">emergency fund</a>, debt payments, upcoming bills,
        housing costs, childcare costs, and normal monthly spending are considered.
      </p>

      <p>
        A trip that fits comfortably should create memories without creating months of stress
        afterward. The safest travel budget leaves room for the experience, the return home,
        and the next financial priority.
      </p>

      <h3>A Simple Travel Budget Rule</h3>

      <p>
        A vacation is usually safer when it is paid mostly from savings, does not wipe out your
        emergency cushion, and does not require high-interest credit card debt. The larger the
        trip, the more important the recovery period becomes.
      </p>

      <h3>Hidden Travel Costs to Include</h3>

      <p>
        Many trips cost more than the first quote. Include luggage, transportation, resort fees,
        tips, meals, snacks, tours, excursions, tickets, travel insurance, passports, parking,
        exchange-rate swings, and emergency spending.
      </p>

      <h3>When Waiting Is the Better Move</h3>

      <p>
        Waiting may be smarter if the trip would drain savings, require credit card debt, delay
        debt payoff, or make normal bills feel tight after you return home. A cheaper version,
        shorter trip, off-season booking, or delayed vacation can protect the same goal with
        less pressure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
  <h2>Travel Planning Guides</h2>

  <p>
    These guides go deeper than the calculators alone, helping you understand hidden travel
    costs, budget pressure points, and ways to avoid vacation debt.
  </p>

  <div class="link-grid">

    <a class="link-card" href="/how-to-budget-for-a-disney-vacation">
      <strong>How to Budget for a Disney Vacation</strong>
      <span>
        Learn how to plan Disney tickets, hotels, food, Lightning Lane-style upgrades,
        souvenirs, and realistic family costs.
      </span>
    </a>
<a class="link-card" href="/how-to-avoid-vacation-debt">
  <strong>How to Avoid Vacation Debt</strong>
  <span>
    Learn how to budget trips realistically, avoid credit card pressure,
    and keep vacations from creating long-term financial stress.
  </span>
</a>
  </div>
</section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Travel Budget FAQ</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How much should I spend on a vacation?</h3>
        <p>
          The right amount depends on income, savings, debt, emergency funds, family size, and
          upcoming obligations. A vacation should not damage your ability to pay normal bills
          or recover financially after the trip.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Should I use savings or finance a vacation?</h3>
        <p>
          Savings are usually safer than financing. High-interest travel debt can turn a good
          trip into months of financial stress after you return home.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What travel costs do people forget?</h3>
        <p>
          Commonly forgotten costs include checked bags, tips, rideshares, parking, resort fees,
          excursions, museum tickets, snacks, phone plans, travel insurance, passports, and
          emergency expenses.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is an expensive vacation ever worth it?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes, an expensive vacation can be worth it if the trip is a real priority, the full
          cost is planned honestly, and the expense does not create debt pressure or drain your
          emergency fund.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How do I make a trip cheaper without ruining it?</h3>
        <p>
          Reduce the number of travel days, choose fewer paid activities, use cheaper dates,
          book lodging with a kitchen, limit souvenirs, avoid unnecessary upgrades, and build
          more free time into the itinerary.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>